In <1993Aug9.154026.70955@cc.usu.edu> sllfl@cc.usu.edu writes:

>In reading through the postings I didn't see any general postings on general
>play and help.  I am just starting the game and I have some questions. 
>	First, how the @#%^$$@# do you find enough food to stay alive? 

Spoilers?

I assume you are not eating the corpses of the critters you have killed?
You should be... at least some of them.  There are poisonous ones to watch
out for (like kobolds).  I found this confusing at first too.  You can
also gain valuable intrinsics this way (like the ability to teleport
or poison resistance).


>Invariably, I start out pretty good and get down to around level 5 or 6 
>only to starve to death.  I don't wear rings, and in fact, I don't see 
>how anyone can wear rings and still stay alive.  

Also remember, if you are getting weak you can #pray and your god will
help you out (assuming that he/she isn't pissed at you).

Praying was something I didn't understand at first either.  I assumed
a god would like someone to pray often... but it didn't seem that way.
In nethack praying is asking your god for help (it's not a form of
worship).  So when the going gets tough and you look like you are going
die... #pray!


>	Second, which characters are good, especially for starting out?  I was
>trying the knight, and two or three times my stats all periodically jumped
>(i.e. I became more intelligent, strong, etc).  Is this only a trait knights
>have or do all the characters benefit from this?

All characters classes generally gain ability with time if they are doing 
things correctly (it is not knight specific).  However, you can lose 
ability too if you are being abusive.  For example, you increase/decrease
your strength, dexterity and constitution depending on how much weight
you are carrying (among other things).

I found that Barbarians are a good beginning character.  They are poison
resistant from the start, so you can go around eating most of the things
you kill w/o too much worry (but keep track of what is poisonous so
you can avoid it when you play as a knight again).


>	Finally, there are a few items that I don't know how to use.  For
>example, what are the magic markers for?  An oracle mentioned something 
>about engraving on the floor.  Do you use markers for this or not, and what 
>good does it do?  

I never tried engraving with a magic marker... who knows.  The main purpose
of a magic marker is to write scrolls.  If you have a blank scroll (or
was washed out scroll made blank) you can write down the names of the
scrolls you have already identified.

As far as 'E'ngraving goes... try gems and wands and see what happens.


>How do you use rings?  

Put them on your finger :-)


>For example, I was trying to use a ring of
>polymorph and I couldn't get it to work.  (I think I was trying to use the
>invoke command (M-i)).  

Give it time.  It happens randomly (like a ring of teleport).  Some
rings are more obvious than others.  You will know when you are wearing
a ring of levitation.  Sinks are good for identifying the less obvious
rings... I'll let you figure out how.


>What are you supposed to do with your pet?  

Pets are great... especially when you are just starting out since they
are probably tougher than you are.  An important thing to know about pets
is that they don't like cursed things.  Take some time to watch what your
pet does when they enter a room... if they avoid an item, then perhaps
you should follow their lead.


>Although it's better with 3.1.3 in that you can "displace" him now instead 
>of waiting for him to move, again the oracle mentioned something about 
>training your pet. 
>How is this possible?

With food!  They seem to like tripe rations the best.  What would you train
your pet to do?  If your not careful, you can train them to not avoid cursed
items (this is bad).  You can train them to bring you things by feeding
them when they drop something near you.  There is a great use for having
a pet know this "trick"... I'll let you figure it out.


